\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Rafi Raja, 35, from Bradford, said he had decided to come after his friend, Malkit Bharj, 57, from east London, intercepted him on his way home from a trip to Tunisia, suggesting they both queue together because they \u8220 love the Queen\u8221 . Raja said the Queen meant a lot to him, because of the memories he had associated with her, including the fact that the first item his mother had received as an engagement gift when she arrived in England after moving from Pakistan was a tea tray with a picture of the queen\u8217 s face on it. He also met her once, aged 9, at a visit to Mumtaz restaurant in Bradford. \u8220 She was a very nice looking lady, very charismatic and polite. Someone told me to curtsy but I didn\u8217 t know how to do it. She tapped my cheek, which was nice of her. I don\u8217 t think the generation that comes next will be as great a person as her,\u8221  he said. Bharj said he had been watching the coverage on TV and, \u8220 felt I must go, I felt a pull\u8221 , and had invited Raja to stay on his couch so he could accompany him. The pair of friends said they haven\u8217 t seen each other since before the pandemic, and that they were enjoying catching up in the queue and meeting others. \u8220 It\u8217 s lovely, lots of nice people,\u8221  said Raja.}
